t(29, 345, """The policewoman is not a security employee .. she is a true reformer on earth. She repairs what is about to be corrupted and shattered! When our sons or daughters go out into the street while they are young, the state system and the whole of society must keep their eyes wide open, because society must understand that teenagers, around the clock, are easy prey for every mistake, expected or unexpected""")

t(29, 1930, """What are you doing here in the street? For half an hour you have been moving from one shop to another? What is your name and where do you live? Come with me""")

t(30, 925, """The society of Medina the Illuminated did not have:
🔴 Night police
🔴 Hospitals working around the clock
🔴 Airports
🔴 Seaports
🔴 Police stations
🔴 Night guards, whether civilian ones in buildings or government ones in front of government institutions and embassies

The society of Medina had none of these features .. and all these people are travelers arriving through airports or seaports, airport and seaport staff, night patrol police, police stations that must never close, hospital staff, emergency room patients, patients' companions, airport staff and others. None of this existed in Medina the Illuminated or in the first era of Islam. Therefore the jurisprudence of reality requires dealing with the variables of life in a balanced, sound and wise manner when we address a juristic issue relating to selling and distributing food at night, or to some or a few restaurants staying open at night.""")

t(30, 1945, """Why are cafés and restaurants not all closed, without exception, immediately after the Isha prayer, given that the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ was that he disliked talk (evening chat) after the Isha prayer?
Because the owners of shops, stores and restaurants, once they have prayed Isha, cannot close down immediately after the prayer. So it is natural that closing comes at least an hour later, until the customers finish their food and pay their bills, the last meals served are finished, and the workers clean the kitchen and the dishes; for it is unthinkable that they leave the kitchen dirty for the next day, so that society is harmed by diseases and epidemics spread. Even so, staying up after Isha is not forbidden, only disliked, and the difference between dislike and prohibition is vast. Dislike calls for advising society; but who can prevent people from walking in the street until one and two in the morning? We are not in a kindergarten. Or who can prevent people from staying up in their homes, or in their villages and farms? These are purely personal matters. The hardliners do not understand religion well and think that applying Islam means everything in it is compulsory. These people have not grasped their religion well, nor understood it. Every city in the world has its places that are not subject to municipal laws, such as homes, farms, river banks where people sit at night, and seashores. All these are places where no state has any business with who stays up sitting there and who does not. But in the Sharia, the issue of the mixing of the sexes in""")

t(31, 55, """those places remains what is subject to the laws; and the possibilities of young men and girls loitering, and similar violations, fall within the remit of the police force in its night duties. When you are in a disciplined Muslim society with penalties for unacceptable violations, it is inconceivable that a man would allow his daughter or his son to loiter at night, or a husband allow his wife to loiter alone at night. As for stores, there are stores that do not close because people need food at any time. And what about visitors who arrive through airports or seaports at late hours, whether they need food from stores working around the clock, or a café inside their hotels, or a restaurant inside the hotel or the airport?
Therefore the jurisprudence of reality must be studied well. Hence a restaurant working at night is consistent with the jurisprudence of reality for those who will need food urgently. And one restaurant in every large residential district selling takeaway meals cannot seat the whole city, so its being open until a late hour does not encourage people to stay up and so miss the prayers.
It is clear that new developments have arisen in the lives of Muslims that call for aiming at what is right and coming as close to it as possible, without extremism and without a laxity and dilution that turn the city into something like a wakeful nightclub with people out in the streets. That is not aiming at what is right and coming close to it; that becomes laxity, looseness and encouraging people to stay up and lose their prayers.""")

t(31, 2095, """Why was the black abaya not imposed on women in the streets?
Because Islam has nothing to do with the form and color of women's clothing. What matters is that the dress or coat does not outline the body and is not tight or fitted at the waist, that the clothes are not transparent, that they cover the whole body, and are not short, showing the legs and arms""")

