t(40, 1650, """Prison .. prison .. has the time drawn near?! Talk about the Names and Attributes on the pulpits and in gatherings, in a way that has set the hearts of Muslims at odds on the channels; they insulted one another, declared one another disbelievers and cursed each other; the children of one qiblah have come to curse one another. If the penalty for that were prison, these people would not have dragged the Ummah and its youth into what distracts it from its future .. Not one Companion asked the Prophet ﷺ what the shape of Allah's hand is like, does it resemble our hands? Exalted is Allah the Almighty, our Lord, in great exaltation above such a question. If only prison .. if it were prison .. the pulpits would not have clamored with what halts the march of the Ummah and divides the hearts of Muslims .. while the enemies applaud .. while the enemies laugh .. while the enemies are pleased, reassured, joyful and delighted!""")

t(41, 960, """What is built for men protects men, and protects women .. Shopping centers are spacious inside .. but they do not have room for both sexes together, even if inside they were the size of a city .. They become very cramped when the two mix in them! They cramp morals, and they cramp the space that is the woman's right, the space of safety .. and they cramp the space of the man, who has the right not to walk looking at the ground to avoid glances, so that he walks stumbling and bumping into walls""")

t(41, 2530, """Between the well of Allah's prophet Yusuf, peace be upon him, and the sea of Allah's prophet Musa, peace be upon him, is this: let no soul say of its distress and the hardship it has seen, “Circumstances made me.” The dryness of the well did not make a tyrant, nor did the water of the sea make a submissive man""")

t(42, 335, """Yusuf's well was dry, and Musa's sea was water! Dryness and water are opposites, so that we learn that dryness did not drown the soul of Allah's prophet Yusuf, peace be upon him, in hardness of heart: he set his brothers free the day Allah gave him victory, and with them set free everyone who had wronged him; nor did water soften the soul of Musa, peace be upon him, regarding the truth: he was firm with the Children of Israel as a discipliner and a shepherd.""")

t(42, 770, """No man should advise a woman in her family problems except the cream of the cream of the righteous of the Ummah, and he can never be found easily. If he is one of the mixed righteous, then some of what he says is taken and some rejected; his words are not considered sacred, and before being accepted they are weighed on the scale of the Book, the Sunnah, reason and natural disposition. And a woman should never advise a man in his private family matters. Not one of the women of the earth is fit to advise a man in his family matters, except a woman to whom all people testify for wisdom, calm, understanding and reason. Otherwise, the interference of every woman who imagines herself reasonable while she is weak in understanding and reason: the ruin of this interference is greater than its good.""")

t(42, 1375, """Our Lord the Almighty, holy are His names, magnified in the heavens and on earth, and exalted in great exaltation, says in His Mighty Book:
(“Is he whose evil deed has been made fair-seeming to him, so that he sees it as good...? Indeed Allah lets go astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. So do not let your soul waste away in grief over them. Indeed Allah knows well what they do.”)

How did people make the doing of wrong fair-seeming to themselves and tempt a single individual with it, so that this one individual liked the wrong, was pleased with it and approved of it? The introduction, then the incident
🔴 The introduction
This noble verse addresses one of the most dangerous matters of the soul, which can be observed in people's speech when they talk. Deed, in its meaning, is wide in senses, many in kinds, rich in descriptions. It is not confined, as most people focus, only to religious matters, with a religious fabrication containing misguidance that a misguided person passes on to another, who has thus fallen into the trial of unsound religious orientations. Rather, deeds are many, and among them is the worldly deed that contradicts the Book and the Sunnah.

A famous personality speaks about the evil deed of people that was made fair-seeming to them and at the same time was made fair-seeming to this personality. Let us look at the fact that the adorning of a deed in the soul does not necessarily spring from the soul as the source of the deed. Rather, Satan may make some act fair-seeming to people and they spread it; a famous personality then reacts to it, and that famous personality, in reacting, does a deed which is its own deed, issuing from it and made fair-seeming to it. So the origin of the adorning of the deed a person does comes from an act that people did, made fair-seeming to themselves and loved, although it is a misguided, wrong deed. This act led to a reaction from one individual at whom that deed was directed, and the reaction of that one individual was also an act he was pleased with, although it was wrong, and he made it fair-seeming to himself. All of this falls under the trial of people by people.

🔴 The incident
A famous person, an Islamic preacher, says: “When I enter gatherings people rush at me, until my shoulders were dislocated from the intensity of people's desire to embrace me. So I said: there is no solution except to bring 5 people to surround me and protect me from the rush of people, due to the intensity of their love for me!”""")

t(43, 75, """🔴 The error
This statement is an affirmation to people that their act is right, an approval of the act, and a marketing of it as permissible and acceptable! Whereas the talk here is about an Islamic preacher; whether a man or a woman does not matter.

🔴 The cause of the error
A preacher means a thinker, and a thinker who addresses people and influences their thought cannot be accepted to see a wrong and not say to people, “This is wrong.” That male preacher, or that female preacher, should have said in this interview: “But I stopped and said to the people: this is contrary to the Sharia. I am merely a man / merely a woman who gives lectures, and this deed of trying to touch me as if I were a Buddha idol is contrary to the Book and the Sunnah. Even the Prophet ﷺ, for all the greatness of his rank, was not treated like this by his Companions; so who am I that this should be done with me?” But the personality that is the subject of this criticism approved the matter and did not direct the crowd to their error, and instead of correcting the thought and the behavior, brought male guards, or female guards, to protect him / to protect her!

This adorning of the reaction occurred from this male / female preacher after those present had made their act toward him / toward her fair-seeming to themselves! People whose evil deed was made fair-seeming to them, and a preacher whose evil deed was made fair-seeming to him / to her

🟢 May Allah have mercy on al-Sha'rawi: when people one day carried his car, he became angry, troubled and stricken with grief. The driver drove off away from the people, and he ordered him to stop in front of a small mosque on the road. Al-Sha'rawi entered the mosque's toilets, took off his jallabiya, his turban and his caftan, and set about cleaning and washing the mosque's toilets with his own hand.""")

t(43, 1345, """Flattery in matters of truth is a trembling hand that does not take hold of the Book with strength, a shaken mind, a troubled spirit and a swaying soul""")

t(43, 1570, """He who flatters in matters of truth has done wrong""")

t(43, 1935, """In Islam there is no such thing as “this is a woman, so be gentle with her” when she spreads error among people. This is empty talk, mixing up Amr with Zayd and confusing matters with one another. This is the religion, the fate of the Ummah and the fate of its sons and daughters; there are no exceptions in it. That any woman should come out and say what is not sound by the Book and the Sunnah, and it be said, “No harm, leave her, she is a woman,” or any of the absurd excuses be put forward that are tied to this speaker being a woman and not a man: this is empty talk.""")

t(43, 2455, """On addressing a part of the hadith of the secure truce and the Great Battle: “Then you and they will fight an enemy behind them, and you will be safe and take spoils”

In this part of the hadith the detail is absent. The common and widespread view among those who speak about this hadith is that this""")

t(44, 60, """enemy, whom the Muslims fight in alliance with the Romans, suffers a crushing defeat, falls entirely, is finished and done away with. Analytically, this requires occupying its land. And as is known, today we are in the age of nationalism and dying for the homeland; with the occupation of this enemy's land it does not logically hold that the outcome would be as the Prophet ﷺ said (safety and spoils), because occupying the land means friction with the people, and this entails daily human losses and attrition, not spoils. Therefore this part, concerning fighting a common enemy that is hostile to the Muslims and the Romans and has expansionist ambitions (China), I understand, and Allah the Exalted is Most High and knows best, in the context of a tactical victory, not a strategic one, such that this campaign clips that enemy's nails, breaks some of the longest and strongest claws of its striking military power, and leaves it still for a period of time, unable to go out from its land. That is why the Prophet ﷺ did not say “you will be victorious”, or “you will triumph”, or “you will conquer their land”, which are the natural phrasings in the noble Prophetic wording when he ﷺ speaks of a complete campaign. For example, he said of Constantinople: “they will enter it and take spoils”, or the hadith “Constantinople will surely be conquered”, or the hadith of the campaign against Rome and its conquest. The phrasings of wars and campaigns in the noble Prophetic wording come to these words when the enemy falls completely. Yet about this alliance war, the subject of this post, the Prophet ﷺ said: “you will be safe and take spoils”, without speaking of a conquest, and this is an unusual phrasing in the noble Prophetic wording about conquests.

Accordingly, my reading of this important part of the hadith is a reading that sees two allied powers breaking some of the strongest ribs of this common enemy without entering all of its land. Perhaps the war will be on the borders of China, and the vanguard of its army will be defeated easily. Perhaps the cause of this easy defeat will be the Romans bringing weapons that the Muslims do not possess and China does not possess, so the crushing defeat befalls the vanguard of China's force on the border; the rulers of China are struck with grief and psychological defeat and so retreat from their expansionist plans, while the Romans are struck with pride and arrogance. Hence the Roman soldier's saying: “The cross has prevailed!” This is an aspect whose meaning I did not reflect on until now, as I write; the reason for the Roman soldier's saying “The cross has prevailed!” had not occurred to me before.""")

t(44, 1455, """It is from the bounty of our Lord the Almighty upon us that He gathered us in a gathering of knowledge. Whoever of the brothers and sisters came and joined this gathering, from its beginning 4 years ago until this hour of ours, through the recommendation of any brother who owns a channel and who commended the gathering and advised joining it: a word of thanks is due, even if it has been much delayed, for four years; but its delay was not intended, it was only life with its lows and highs. May Allah reward with good every owner of a channel, or an account on Twitter, or a platform whatever it may be, that pointed people to this purely scholarly gathering.""")

t(44, 2015, """I was asked a question about the Imam a day ago! Does he need the people after he is charged with his mission, or are they the ones who need him? The question is thorny, because the word “need” in itself has classifications. For all human beings without exception are in need of Allah the Exalted; they need Him, holy are His names, with a complete, absolute and total need. But need in another classification takes a different course from the need that is of the kind of the creature's need for the Creator. The infant needs his mother to nurse him, the child needs his parents to care for him, and the director of a government institution needs the employees to get the work done.

The person who asked me the question perhaps meant the sense of the creature who is charged with a general mission and therefore needs aides and helpers to assist him in carrying out the mission. Our Lord the Almighty, holy are His names, magnified in the heavens and on earth, and exalted in great exaltation, said in His Mighty Book about the Prophet ﷺ: (“He it is Who supported you with His help and with the believers”).

It is clear that the believers, in the system of public work in the life of states and societies governed by leaders, are an aid""")

t(45, 60, """and a support, and this is something on which there is no disagreement. Even the prophets, blessings and peace be upon them, were not excepted from this matter. But many people do not distinguish between the prophets, blessings and peace be upon them, and the Mahdi, peace be upon him, in terms of role. The role of the prophets and messengers, blessings and peace be upon them, on earth is religious in the first place, and the believers' support of them comes from the spring of religion and the root of its tree. A prophet is not supported, in principle, in order to run a state; he is supported to spread the religion of monotheism. Excepted from this context are prophets such as Allah's prophet Sulayman and Allah's prophet Yusuf, then our Prophet ﷺ, and, among those whose prophethood is not established by text, Dhul-Qarnayn. Here we are speaking of the side of rule and managing people's affairs.

Most people, though not all of them, still do not distinguish between the tasks of the prophets whose origin and basis is religion, and the tasks of rule, as was the case with Allah's prophet Sulayman, peace be upon him, and Dhul-Qarnayn. In the type of rule of those two there are clear orientations toward the context of governing, the subjects and judging between people, not a new message of religion and monotheism that must be conveyed to people. The basis of the work of the Prophet ﷺ was spreading monotheism, and the features of the state were not complete in the forms that would make the Prophet ﷺ possess a state with organized structures, unlike the experience of Allah's prophet Sulayman, peace be upon him, and before him his father Dawud, peace be upon him, and the experience of Dhul-Qarnayn.

From here we must understand that the last coming experience before Allah's prophet Isa, peace be upon him, is not a religious experience, nor a prophetic, message-bearing effort that the coming person will undertake. It is rather an experience of internal and external rule. The experience of that person has a special character, whose cause is not only the report that came about him, that he does not rejoice at one who comes nor grieve over one who leaves; although that is a report that agrees, in reason and logic, with the strength of soul that Allah the Exalted has granted that man. But following the context of his stage, understanding the reality of his mission, and realizing that he is inspired and spoken to, before the mission and after it, lets us understand a truth absent from minds and grasped by none (but those who know): that the coming personality possesses such complete trust in Allah the Exalted as puts him in a state of certainty that everyone who turns his back and goes is replaced, that everyone who comes forward is the one made successor, that the Ummah is brimming with capabilities, and that it will not fail to have within it thousands of those able to bring about change in that time.

The question the questioner raised, and with which I conclude, is: what is the reason for his indifference toward the one who comes forward, and his indifference toward the one who turns away? This too is undoubtedly a question whose answer is grasped only by those who know, and none knows their number but Allah the Exalted. The answer is that this man, out of certainty in Allah the Exalted, believes that his role and his task will be completed in the most perfect way under all circumstances and according to all scenarios, whether people come forward or turn away. The outcome with him is fixed and not open to doubt. This is not a method that was absent from the prophets. They too, even though their effort was prophetic, message-bearing and one of conveying, would never doubt that everyone who comes forward comes for his own sake, not theirs, and that everyone who turns away does them no harm, because Allah the Exalted will complete His light on earth, complete His command and complete what He wills. This understanding makes anyone charged with a task on earth who strives for truth, good and justice, whether a king, a president, the director of a government institution or a minister, whatever the title may be, see nothing to worry him in people's coming to him and turning away from him. So what do you think of the last one charged before Allah's prophet Isa, peace be upon him, who is given victory from heaven and from earth?! We are speaking of a human being who, when he speaks, is right; when he relates, is truthful; when he judges, is just; and when asked, gives the correct answer at 101%, not even 100%, in the questions and cases that come before him. It seems to me that people's ignorance of him is what will keep them away, since they think he is al-Sha'rawi, may Allah have mercy on him, or Ertugrul, or Salah al-Din, or one of the renewer personalities at the head of every hundred years. The cause of this ignorance of him is that they have not understood that the Ummah's spirit has melted away and its real role has fallen for 1407 years, and that this human being will be a cause of the Ummah's rising after 1407 years, not after 100 years. They do not grasp these meanings and think they are dealing with a slight and easy matter. So whoever is ignorant of the reality of his mission scatters from him, and whoever understands the reality of his mission comes to him. On the day he is charged, none will come to him except one who has understood these words I have now written, taken them in with his heart, not his mind, grasped well the magnitude of their meanings and held to them.""")
